    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d270">

                <pb facs="0267" n="257" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg257"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European War 1939/11–944:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kennan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-11-09T17:00:00+03:00">November 9, 1944—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4289. The morning press contains a Tass item from Bucharest to the effect that
                    Vyshinski arrived Taure [<hi rendition="#italic">there</hi>] on the 8th to check
                    up on Rumania’s execution of the armistice terms.</p>
                <pb facs="0268" n="258" xml:id="pg258"/>
                <p>Neither the British Ambassador nor I had any advance warning of this visit, nor
                    any knowledge of his departure prior to the appearance of this press item.</p>
                <p>Considering especially the fact that we were technically in communication with
                    him on matters connected with execution of the armistice terms in Rumania, we
                    were a little taken aback by this news.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
